This might feel weird because after all,

01:21.240 --> 01:23.960
isn't this what we've been doing this entire time?

01:24.600 --> 01:27.440
The secret to this lies in the fact

01:27.440 --> 01:29.720
that what we talked about when we covered setup,

01:29.720 --> 01:33.640
which is that everything in here is vanilla JavaScript.

01:33.640 --> 01:35.680
So what you're returning here,

01:35.680 --> 01:39.320
while view will go ahead and register it to the rest of the component,

01:39.320 --> 01:41.760
there's no reactivity attached to it right now.

01:41.760 --> 01:44.040
Because why? It's a plain JavaScript const.

01:44.040 --> 01:46.780
It's a constant, and the same thing is happening with Pokedex.

01:46.780 --> 01:49.440
In other words, all the stuff that we were talking about regarding

01:49.440 --> 01:50.960
reactive data, computer properties,

01:50.960 --> 01:53.320
that will not work with this because at this point,

01:53.320 --> 01:54.400
it's not reactive.

01:54.400 --> 01:57.520
So how do we bring some reactivity?

01:57.520 --> 02:01.760
Well, the way we do that is actually by using view helper methods.

02:01.760 --> 02:03.360
So we mentioned that earlier,

02:03.360 --> 02:08.000
where a really popular thing now with view3 is the ability to parse out

02:08.000 --> 02:11.040
different functions to help people use it in different contexts.

02:11.040 --> 02:13.160
Well, there is a helper method

02:13.160 --> 02:15.440
for turning something into a reactive reference.

02:15.440 --> 02:19.200
The way we do that is we import from view,

02:19.200 --> 02:23.040
and we destructure out this method called ref.

02:23.280 --> 02:26.560
Again, if you think of ref, think of reactive reference.

02:26.560 --> 02:28.640
So how does that work? Well, what we do is,

02:28.640 --> 02:30.800
we actually just wrap the value that we want to be

02:30.800 --> 02:33.920
reactive inside of this helper method.

02:33.920 --> 02:36.040
That's what we do.

02:36.040 --> 02:39.240
So now if I refresh this and we change it,

02:39.240 --> 02:42.480
you'll notice it's now actually being tracked by view.

02:42.480 --> 02:46.800
So this ref and a lot of the concepts we'll be talking about,

02:46.800 --> 02:49.060
is actually really critical because going forward,

02:49.060 --> 02:51.000
whenever you're working inside of composition API,

02:51.080 --> 02:54.640
you have to be very explicit about when you want view to introduce magic.

02:54.640 --> 02:57.480
So this is actually one of the things I do think that's beneficial to

02:57.480 --> 02:59.240
the composition API is because now,

02:59.240 --> 03:02.040
you know exactly where view is coming in to do things.

03:02.040 --> 03:04.080
Whereas in the options API,

03:04.080 --> 03:05.760
you could argue that the this context could be

03:05.760 --> 03:07.800
confusing for JavaScript developers and what if they

03:07.800 --> 03:09.600
wanted to come in and change some stuff, right?

03:09.600 --> 03:13.160
Composition API is your ability to get at the primitives and say,

03:13.160 --> 03:14.680
okay, this is the stuff I want to do,

03:14.680 --> 03:17.560
and then view, do your magic for this kind of stuff.

03:17.560 --> 03:19.720
So for example, if we wanted to say,

03:19.920 --> 03:23.480
let's actually have ability to have like an all caps region name.

03:23.480 --> 03:26.680
Well, the way we could easily do that is you can have a computer property.

03:26.680 --> 03:28.040
That's how I would approach this.

03:28.040 --> 03:30.960
So we'll say region all caps and

03:30.960 --> 03:33.560
actually I'll just be more specific region name all caps.

03:33.560 --> 03:34.280
And what do we need how?

03:34.280 --> 03:36.400
We need a computer property and guess what?

03:36.400 --> 03:38.000
The helper method is computed.

03:39.400 --> 03:43.560
So we have a computer property, but what we need to do inside of our computer

03:43.560 --> 03:45.480
property is we need to run a function.

03:45.480 --> 03:48.480
Because if we look back at our other apps that use computed,

03:48.480 --> 03:50.280
let's go back into another component that uses it.

03:52.560 --> 03:55.120
You'll see that all of our computer properties actually are functions,

03:55.120 --> 03:59.400
which makes sense because they need to run a computation on the things that are

03:59.400 --> 04:01.040
the dependencies.

04:01.040 --> 04:04.520
So we have an arrow function in here and what are we going to do?

04:04.520 --> 04:07.360
We're going to go ahead and we're going to return just like we did before.

04:07.360 --> 04:10.800
We're going to turn the region name to uppercase.

04:10.800 --> 04:14.560
This is probably our first instinct as far as how we would make this work.

04:14.560 --> 04:15.640
And so to make sure this works,

04:15.640 --> 04:23.000
we got to make sure we return this to region name all caps just like that.

04:23.000 --> 04:26.440
And then let's go ahead and expose that here as an H3.

04:26.440 --> 04:31.480
Region name all caps just like that and we'll save.

04:31.480 --> 04:35.600
And you might notice that, well, something's broken.

04:35.600 --> 04:36.200
What's going on?

04:38.240 --> 04:40.800
Well, it's saying region name by two uppercase is not a function.

04:40.800 --> 04:45.280
You might be thinking, this is weird, but region name is a string, right?

04:46.120 --> 04:46.560
It's not.

04:47.680 --> 04:49.320
Yeah, it's not a string anymore.

04:49.320 --> 04:53.200
It is now a reactive reference, which actually some of you have noticed when

04:53.200 --> 04:55.720
you're doing the inspecting of the element.

04:55.720 --> 04:59.960
Let's go ahead and actually log region name at this point and see what we get.

05:01.800 --> 05:04.720
If I refresh that, that's fine, we get a massive error.

05:04.720 --> 05:07.840
And you'll see things like the ref implementation.

05:09.280 --> 05:12.520
You also might have seen as well when you're doing the options API,

05:12.520 --> 05:15.160
you saw proxies show up when you would log out of value.

05:15.160 --> 05:16.920
And I know that was confusing at first to some people because they're

05:16.920 --> 05:19.760
expecting to just see what the value actually is.

05:19.760 --> 05:23.600
But one of the big rewrites of view three when it came to the reactivity engine

05:23.600 --> 05:27.080
is that it's using the JavaScript proxies underneath in order to manage how

05:27.080 --> 05:29.000
all the dependencies are being tracked.

05:29.000 --> 05:31.760
And so again, you don't need to understand how proxies work for

05:31.760 --> 05:35.920
you to be familiar with or basically to work with view.

05:35.920 --> 05:38.720
But in case that's something you're interested in, I just wanted to give you

05:38.720 --> 05:40.880
sort of like a little bit of a preview of that so

05:40.880 --> 05:43.600
that you can always dive into that if you want to learn more about that.

05:43.600 --> 05:47.960
But you'll notice here that once again, region name is not a string.

05:47.960 --> 05:48.480
What is it?

05:48.480 --> 05:50.880
It's a ref implementation because it's a reactive reference.

05:50.880 --> 05:55.120
Meaning the way we actually get to the value of it is actually to basically

05:55.120 --> 05:56.720
think of it as an object now.

05:56.720 --> 05:59.320
It's an object that has the ability to update it, it's dependencies.

05:59.320 --> 06:02.360
And so when we actually want to access the raw value of a reference,

06:02.360 --> 06:04.600
we have to actually unpack the thing.

06:04.600 --> 06:06.960
The way we do that is by saying dot value.

06:08.960 --> 06:11.440
And you'll notice once you do that, everything works.

06:12.240 --> 06:16.280
Now, this might feel a little bit odd after using the options API for

06:16.280 --> 06:20.640
a little while because after all in the past, or actually not even in the past,

06:20.640 --> 06:26.240
if we wanted to, we could have theoretically created a computer property at this level.

06:26.240 --> 06:31.720
And just for the sake of having comparison, this will be region name lowercase.

06:31.720 --> 06:36.720
And so what we would do here is return this.regionname.to lowercase.

06:36.720 --> 06:38.440
Just like that.

06:38.920 --> 06:44.200
And then we'll just add another h3.regionname all.

06:44.200 --> 06:47.480
Okay, no, no, it looks like it's just lowercase, yeah.

06:47.480 --> 06:48.760
Okay, save.

06:48.760 --> 06:52.720
Okay, so that happens to work.

06:52.720 --> 06:55.480
Let me have a differentiator here.

06:55.480 --> 06:56.240
There we go.

06:56.240 --> 06:58.680
Okay, sentence case, all case, okay.

06:59.760 --> 07:04.040
So you might be thinking, well, this is way nicer, right?

07:04.040 --> 07:05.720
Like why do I want dot value?

07:05.720 --> 07:08.920
And I'll say when I first was exposed to this, I would agree.

07:08.920 --> 07:10.840
It definitely feels odd.

07:10.840 --> 07:14.480
But there is something to understand about the reason why this is actually

07:14.480 --> 07:18.200
helpful is because we do have to remember that when you're working in vanilla

07:18.200 --> 07:20.720
JavaScript, it is important to be able to tell when something is viewed,

07:20.720 --> 07:22.520
when something is not.

07:22.520 --> 07:24.600
And so we think about const region name.

07:24.600 --> 07:27.400
The fact that you actually have to unpack the value does signify to people that

07:27.400 --> 07:28.880
when they're working with this particular value,

07:28.880 --> 07:30.960
that it's already attached to the view reactivity.

07:32.560 --> 07:35.480
That said, I wanted to give you the pros of how people see that because

07:35.480 --> 07:36.920
a lot of people do like ref.

07:36.920 --> 07:40.600
But in the event you're like, I really don't like ref.

07:40.600 --> 07:45.280
What you can do instead is you can do this helper method called reactive.

07:46.960 --> 07:52.200
And so what reactive is, is basically think of the data option from

07:52.200 --> 07:56.080
the options API packaged in a nice little helper function.

07:56.080 --> 07:58.920
Because earlier, there was a question regarding around the fact that,

07:58.920 --> 08:00.680
what if I don't like the name data?

08:00.680 --> 08:01.760
What if I want to call it state?

08:01.760 --> 08:03.160
Well, we can.

08:03.160 --> 08:06.880
In this case, state, it's a reactive object.

08:06.880 --> 08:13.080
And inside of here, let's just say, I'll just do like region two.

08:14.200 --> 08:17.800
And I actually don't know a third region.

08:17.800 --> 08:21.960
It's fine, okay, I'll just do element type and let's say lightning.

08:23.560 --> 08:27.600
Okay, so when you're doing the state, first of all,

08:27.600 --> 08:30.080
I'll go ahead and log out state.

08:33.200 --> 08:37.120
Once you end up getting is something that looks like a lot more of what people

08:37.120 --> 08:39.880
are probably expecting when they're actually inspecting element,

08:39.880 --> 08:41.600
which is the proxy.

08:41.600 --> 08:46.120
This is what we've seen earlier when we actually inspected the options API

08:46.120 --> 08:49.880
element in that you have to look at the target in order to get underneath it.

08:49.880 --> 08:54.640
But what's nice about this now is that, again, this is gonna be misplaced.

08:54.640 --> 09:01.800
But if we do state.element type.to uppercase and run it,

09:01.800 --> 09:03.760
you'll see it actually just works.

09:05.600 --> 09:11.680
And so the way to think of reactive is it's like a nicely packaged way of

09:11.680 --> 09:14.560
collecting your refs together and that you don't have to unpackage it with dot value.

09:15.640 --> 09:19.200
And so there are plenty of code bases I've seen that people never touch ref.

09:19.200 --> 09:25.640
They like reactive, because reactive feels like options API,

09:25.640 --> 09:28.280
but you're in the composition API setting.

09:29.280 --> 09:31.120
And so is there a right or wrong way to do this?

09:31.120 --> 09:32.400
Absolutely not.

09:32.400 --> 09:35.240
So for the sake of having this example also be here,

09:35.240 --> 09:37.880
I'm just gonna say element type all caps.

09:37.880 --> 09:42.040
And then we'll make sure to return this here, make it alphabetical.

09:43.600 --> 09:47.880
And then I'll just make sure it's here and then we should be good, okay?

09:51.360 --> 09:53.480
So there we have it.

09:53.480 --> 09:57.840
This is the fundamentals around composition API.

09:57.840 --> 10:00.600
As far as the highlights to consider for this once again,

10:00.600 --> 10:03.160
is that you're now in free form JavaScript land.

10:03.160 --> 10:07.560
So this is where I would say comparing options API to composition API is that

10:07.560 --> 10:11.240
options API, you don't need as much JavaScript to get up and running, I would argue.

10:11.240 --> 10:14.760
And I've seen that before with people who have just enough to understand the syntax,

10:14.760 --> 10:18.160
where because of the this syntax, they were able to just kind of get in there,

10:18.160 --> 10:20.080
make some changes, make some swaps.

10:20.080 --> 10:23.120
And the templating was easy enough that people without a lot of JavaScript

10:23.120 --> 10:25.400
experience could actually be productive on a code base.

10:26.400 --> 10:30.000
With something like this, however, when it comes to the composition API,

10:30.000 --> 10:31.480
as you can see, there's a lot more going on.

10:31.480 --> 10:34.040
You need to understand helper functions, you need to know about modules,

10:34.040 --> 10:36.800
you need to go ahead and make sure you know if you're using ref that it's a dot

10:36.800 --> 10:40.560
value that the skill cap jumps considerably when it comes to the amount of

10:40.560 --> 10:45.800
JavaScript you need to know in order to be productive with composition API.

